                    "topic": "Cellular Respiration",
                    "fileName": "Text Input",
                    "text": "Cellular Respiration\r\n\nCellular respiration is the process cells use to extract usable energy from nutrients such as glucose. In aerobic respiration, oxygen is used and carbon dioxide and water are produced.\r\n\nThe main stages of cellular respiration are glycolysis, the citric acid cycle, and oxidative phosphorylation.\r\n\nGlycolysis occurs in the cytoplasm. During glycolysis, one glucose molecule is broken down into two molecules of pyruvate. A small amount of ATP and NADH is produced.\r\n\nThe citric acid cycle occurs in the mitochondrial matrix in eukaryotic cells. It completes the breakdown of carbon compounds and produces ATP, NADH, and FADH2.\r\n\nOxidative phosphorylation occurs at the inner mitochondrial membrane. Electrons from NADH and FADH2 move through an electron transport chain.\r\n\nThe electron transport chain uses the energy released by electrons to pump hydrogen ions across the inner mitochondrial membrane. This creates a concentration gradient.\r\n\nATP synthase uses the movement of hydrogen ions through the membrane to produce ATP. Oxygen serves as the final electron acceptor and combines with hydrogen ions to form water.\r\n\nMost ATP produced during aerobic cellular respiration comes from oxidative phosphorylation.\r\n\nWhen oxygen is unavailable, some cells can use fermentation to continue producing small amounts of ATP through glycolysis."
