                    "text": "Photosynthesis\r\n\nPhotosynthesis is the process by which plants, algae, and some bacteria convert light energy into chemical energy.\r\n\nIn plants, photosynthesis primarily occurs in chloroplasts. Chloroplasts contain a green pigment called chlorophyll that absorbs light energy.\r\n\nPhotosynthesis uses carbon dioxide and water as reactants. Light energy provides the energy needed to produce glucose and oxygen.\r\n\nThe overall process can be summarized as carbon dioxide plus water, using light energy, produces glucose and oxygen.\r\n\nPhotosynthesis occurs in two major stages: the light-dependent reactions and the Calvin cycle.\r\n\nThe light-dependent reactions occur in the thylakoid membranes of chloroplasts. Light energy is used to produce ATP and NADPH. Water is split during these reactions, releasing oxygen as a byproduct.\r\n\nThe Calvin cycle occurs in the stroma of the chloroplast. It uses ATP and NADPH to help convert carbon dioxide into carbohydrates.\r\n\nGlucose produced through photosynthesis can be used as an energy source or stored for later use.\r\n\nPhotosynthesis is important because it converts solar energy into chemical energy and produces much of the oxygen used by aerobic organisms.\r\n\nThe rate of photosynthesis can be affected by factors such as light intensity, carbon dioxide concentration, temperature, and water availability."
