                    "text": "DNA and Genetics\r\n\nDNA, or deoxyribonucleic acid, contains the genetic instructions used by living organisms. DNA is made of smaller units called nucleotides.\r\n\nEach nucleotide contains a sugar, a phosphate group, and a nitrogenous base. The four bases in DNA are adenine, thymine, cytosine, and guanine.\r\n\nAdenine pairs with thymine, while cytosine pairs with guanine. These complementary base pairs help DNA maintain its structure and allow genetic information to be copied accurately.\r\n\nGenes are sections of DNA that contain instructions for producing functional products such as proteins. Different versions of a gene are called alleles.\r\n\nChromosomes are structures made of DNA and proteins. Humans normally have 46 chromosomes arranged into 23 pairs.\r\n\nDuring DNA replication, the DNA molecule separates and each original strand serves as a template for a new strand. This produces two DNA molecules that are nearly identical to the original.\r\n\nTranscription is the process of copying information from DNA into messenger RNA. Translation uses the information in messenger RNA to build a protein.\r\n\nMutations are changes in DNA sequences. Some mutations have little or no effect, while others can change the function of a protein or affect an organism's traits.\r\n\nGenotype refers to the genetic makeup of an organism. Phenotype refers to the observable characteristics produced by the interaction of genes and the environment.\r\n\nGregor Mendel studied inheritance using pea plants. His experiments helped establish basic principles of genetics, including the idea that organisms inherit genetic information from their parents."
