                    "text": "Memory\n \u007f Memory is the ability to encode, store, and retrieve information.\n\u007f Psychologists study memory to understand how information is learned, retained, and\nlater accessed.\n\nThe Three Basic Processes\n \u007f Encoding: transforming information into a form that can be stored.\n\u007f Storage: maintaining information over time.\n\u007f Retrieval: bringing stored information back when it is needed.\n\nSensory Memory\n \u007f Sensory memory briefly holds information received through the senses.\n\u007f It allows the brain to retain a very short record of sights, sounds, and other sensory\ninformation.\n\u007f Most sensory information disappears quickly unless attention is directed toward it.\n\nShort-Term and Working Memory\n \u007f Short-term memory holds a limited amount of information for a short period.\n\u007f Working memory allows information to be temporarily held and manipulated while\ncompleting a task.\n\u007f Repeating information can help keep it active.\n\nLong-Term Memory\n \u007f Long-term memory can store information for much longer periods.\n\u007f Explicit memory involves information that can be consciously recalled.\n\u007f Implicit memory influences behavior without requiring conscious recall.\n\nEncoding and Rehearsal\n \u007f Rehearsal involves repeating information to help maintain it in memory.\n\u007f Elaborative rehearsal connects new information to knowledge that is already stored.\n\u007f Making meaningful connections can make information easier to remember.\n\nWhy Do We Forget?\n \u007f Forgetting can happen when information was not encoded successfully.\n\u007f Memories can also become difficult to retrieve.\n\u007f Interference occurs when other information makes it harder to remember something.\n\u007f New information can interfere with older memories, and older information can interfere\nwith newer learning.\n\nRecall vs. Recognition\n \u007f Recall requires retrieving information without the same type of direct prompt.\n\u007f Recognition involves identifying information that has been encountered before.\n\u007f Recognition is often easier because the available information provides additional\ncues.\n\nMemory and Sleep\n \u007f Sleep is associated with memory consolidation.\n\u007f Consolidation is the process through which newly learned information becomes\nmore stable in long-term memory.\n\u007f Sleep can therefore play an important role in learning and remembering.\n\nKey Terms\n \u007f Encoding: converting information into a form that can be stored.\n\u007f Storage: maintaining information over time.\n\u007f Retrieval: accessing stored information.\n\u007f Working memory: temporarily holding and using information.\n\u007f Consolidation: stabilizing newly learned information."
